meta {
  name: driver-onboarding
}

docs {
  # Driver Self-Onboarding Flow

  Full happy path for a new driver registering independently (not added by any truck owner
  first): OTP login → self-register + DL auto-verify → (manual DL upload if auto-verify is
  exhausted) → get driver code → add a payout method.

  Run requests 1–7 in order (use the "staging" environment, top-right). Each request sets the
  environment variables the next one needs — `accessToken`, `driverId`, `dlVerificationOutcome`,
  `driverCode` — so as long as you run them in sequence you shouldn't need to copy anything by
  hand.

  Step 4 is conditional — see its own docs. Steps 6 and 7 are alternatives to each other, not
  a required pair.

  For any single endpoint outside this specific journey (admin approve/reject, set-default
  payout method, driver-torg linking, etc.), see the `plutus-api` reference collection under
  `bruno/collections/` instead of this flow.

  ## Steps 8–14: BUG-178 regression — a UPI can't be silently re-mapped to another driver

  These steps are a separate check appended to this journey rather than a required part of the
  happy path: they log in as a TORG_ADMIN (`torgAdminPhone`/`torgAdminOtp`) and confirm
  `VPAService` refuses to hand a driver's UPI to a second driver
  (`VPAResource.java`/`VPAService.createVPAForDriver`, MBT-178/BUG-178). Run 1–5 first so
  `{{driverId}}` is set; steps 6/7 are not required first.

  Step 10 exists because step 7 posts to a URL (`POST /api/vpas/verify` with a `vpa` body field)
  that doesn't match any route in the current `VPAResource` — the real single-call "add UPI"
  endpoint is `POST /api/vpas` with `{vpa, driverId}` in the body, which is also the only VPA
  write endpoint the DRIVER role itself is allowed to call. Step 10 uses that corrected shape
  (via the admin token, which is also allowed on it) to reliably give driver A the UPI these
  steps depend on, since step 7 does not.

  Also worth knowing for every assertion here: `ApiResponseFilter` rewrites the wire-level HTTP
  status of every `api/*` response to 200 — both success (200/201/204 all become 200) and a
  `BadRequestException`/422 (also forced to 200). The real outcome lives in the JSON body:
  `success`/`statusCode` always, plus `message` for an error. Only a handful of statuses (403,
  404, 409, 5xx) are left alone. `res.status: eq 200` in these assertions is expected, not a
  mistake.

  As of this writing (2026-09-28), the fix is on plutus-java branch
  `fix/BUG-178-vpa-driver-remapping` (commit f287ce55e) and has **not** been deployed to staging.
  Steps 12 and 14 are written against the fixed behavior and will fail against current staging —
  step 12 would come back `success: true` with the UPI silently reassigned to the second driver,
  and step 13's list-check would then find it missing from driver A's list. Re-run once the fix
  ships.
}
